WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:06.319
Now, as I already said, half the country and
99 percent of the world breathed a huge sigh

00:00:06.319 --> 00:00:11.420
of relief that Donald Trump is being removed
from office strongly and powerfully being

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:12.510
removed.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:17.230
And plenty of people, understandably, are
exhausted from the last four years and they're

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:20.700
looking to tune out of politics now for a
while.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.240
This is not the time to do it.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:26.040
And in fact, this is when the activism can
start.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:29.340
We will be talking about the Georgia Senate
races.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.869
Control of the Senate has not yet been decided.

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:36.550
We have two special elections on January 5th
that will decide control of the Senate.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:41.610
But this is much bigger than that now is when
you have to get to work to, number one, undo

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.829
the disasters of Trump.

00:00:43.829 --> 00:00:46.670
And number two, more importantly, we have
to do stuff.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:50.640
We on the left believe that government should
do some things.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.760
The right has the structural advantage because
one of their ideas is government shouldn't

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:56.070
be doing anything.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:01:00.640
Now, of course, if they need to ban gay marriage,
all of a sudden they should be doing something

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.110
if they need to ban abortion.

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:06.649
But other than when they're hypocrites, their
idea is get government out.

00:01:06.649 --> 00:01:10.030
They win by preventing government from doing
anything.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:12.909
Now is when we have to start trying to put
points on the board.

00:01:12.909 --> 00:01:15.070
I've used this analogy before.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:18.100
We've been playing defense while Donald Trump
has been president.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.969
They've had the ball.

00:01:19.969 --> 00:01:23.289
We've gotten the ball back by having Joe Biden
win.

00:01:23.289 --> 00:01:28.390
But Biden may not have the Senate and simply
getting Trump out doesn't win.

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:33.229
We will be discussing Biden's plans for day
one executive orders shortly.

00:01:33.229 --> 00:01:34.579
And that list has to grow.

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:38.459
But the real systemic changes won't come through
executive orders.

00:01:38.459 --> 00:01:45.049
They will come through legislation, tax reform,
climate policy, health care, education, coronavirus

00:01:45.049 --> 00:01:46.049
relief.

00:01:46.049 --> 00:01:47.609
These things will happen through legislation.

00:01:47.609 --> 00:01:51.740
And without Senate control, it will be really
tough, which means, number one, we must be

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:55.109
laser focused for the next two months on the
Senate races.

00:01:55.109 --> 00:01:58.579
And number two, this is the time to engage.

00:01:58.579 --> 00:02:01.100
So I'm not underselling Biden's win.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.469
It's huge.

00:02:02.469 --> 00:02:04.969
Removing an incumbent president is really
tough.

00:02:04.969 --> 00:02:11.310
But I want to remind you, Biden's win is the
prerequisite to being able to be activists

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:12.630
for policy.

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:17.470
During the Trump presidency, we were activists
for removing Trump to stop the bleeding and

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.880
preventing him from doing more damage.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:23.440
Now we actually have to be activists for doing
the work of getting things done.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:28.890
I was stunned and inspired that we had during
our our late last week streams.

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:34.020
And over the weekend, about a thousand new
members sign up on our website with the coupon

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:38.500
code Fresh Start, the idea being we are now
at the start.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.600
It's a new start and an important one at that.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.630
This is how progress is made.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:49.820
You think to the big progressive eras, the
three big areas of progressive change, the

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:56.210
progressive era of 1890 to 1920, the New Deal
era towards the end of World War Two, the

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:59.880
civil rights era of the late fifties into
the sixties.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.460
The presidential elections were merely the
beginnings.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:03.630
They were the prerequisites.

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:10.300
But the successes depended on voting and voting
again and again, and on protests and activism,

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:16.190
walkouts and sit ins, lobbying elected officials,
getting the right people on the Supreme Court.

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:21.170
Sometimes activism requires pushing cases
through courts higher and higher through the

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:22.180
appeals process.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.470
And right now we certainly don't have.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:30.750
The Supreme Court, so the point is, we're
tired, the last four years have been exhausting,

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.790
we have two months now with a really simple
focus.

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:38.990
Get the two Senate seats in Georgia will then
have a sort of two week break.

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:42.440
And then Joe Biden is inaugurated and the
real work starts.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:45.040
I hope you'll be with me for it.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:48.209
Let me know on Twitter at David Pakman what
you believe the focus should be

